      "arabicLemma": "قوله وقد يطلق الصدق والحق",
      "english": "The observation that ṣidq and ḥaqq can also name the abstract property of correspondence answers an anticipated objection. Earlier, ṣidq was explained as a report which corresponds to the real state of affairs and falsehood as a report which does not. But a proposition is subsequently defined as a statement capable of truth or falsehood. If truth and falsehood there mean *true report* and *false report*, the explanation becomes “a proposition is a statement capable of being a corresponding report or a noncorresponding report.” Because “proposition” and “report” here are effectively coextensive, the definition appears to employ what is being defined in its own definition, producing circularity.\n\nThe response is that ṣidq can name not only a report which corresponds but also the *correspondence itself*, while falsehood can name the corresponding absence of correspondence. In defining the proposition, the latter meanings are intended, and circularity is avoided.\n\nAn objection remains: the same words now have several significations. Using an equivocal term without qualification in a definition is generally defective. It might be replied that the property of correspondence is the literal, verbal-noun sense of ṣidq and that applying it to a true report is a metaphor, as in “Zayd is justice”; when a literal meaning is available, one need not choose the figurative one.\n\nTwo difficulties attend that answer. First, ṣidq has already been described as the act of reporting something as it is—a quality of the speaker—so using it for an abstract relation of correspondence may itself be figurative. Second, even if calling a corresponding report “truth” originated as a metaphor, usage among scholars is now so frequent that this sense comes to mind without any special indicator. Such conventional prominence cannot simply be ignored. A more convincing resolution is that, although a figurative signification can become as familiar as a literal one, the fact that taking it here would make the definition defective is itself an indication that the other intended sense is operative.",
