      "arabicLemma": "قوله أو مستقر",
      "english": "The word mustaqarr is the passive participle of “settled within,” originally with a complement which is suppressed through the frequency of the expression, just as an expression originally meaning “shared in” may be abbreviated to “shared.” A stable or predicative adverbial (ẓarf mustaqarr) is one whose governing expression has been omitted, whether the omitted action is general or particular. An ordinary or laghw adverbial has its governing expression present. This is the main commentator's preferred distinction; hence he attaches the expression to an understood “being connected with,” which is not one of the verbs of the most general existence.\n\nThe four commonly cited general verbal concepts are being, existing, standing or remaining established, and occurring or obtaining, as represented in a Persian mnemonic verse. Some add settling and its derivatives. The others count as particular activities. A further distinction, attributed to a learned Yemenite grammarian, is that when no specific contextual indication is present, the omitted governor is reconstructed as general; where the context points to a particular action, that more informative particular verb should be understood.",
