      "arabicLemma": "قوله أو مستقر خبر لمبتدأ",
      "english": "The discussion begins with a question about why the second clause alone receives the parsing “a stable adverbial serving as a predicate of a subject.” The first clause could also be construed that way, and the difference in grammatical treatment needs an explanation.\n\nThe ordinary rule is that a laghw adverbial is related to a governor explicitly stated, while a mustaqarr adverbial has an omitted governor. The former can be called “idle” because it exercises no independent syntactic operation, neither overtly nor in an implied pronoun; or because its governor's pronoun has not “settled” within it. In a sentence such as “Zayd is in the house,” the verb understood as its governor is absent, and its pronominal relation is said to have settled in the prepositional phrase.\n\nAnother tradition distinguishes general existential verbs from verbs specifying a particular activity: the predicative adverbial would have a general governor (being, existing, obtaining), while the ordinary adjunct would depend on a specific action, whether that action is actually expressed or omitted. This differs from simply classifying by the presence or absence of the governor.\n\nA passage attributed to al-Sayyid al-Sharīf in his marginal notes on al-Kashshāf complicates the matter. An omitted governor need not always be a general existential verb. If one says “Zayd on the horse,” “Zayd among the scholars” or “Zayd in Basra,” the intended activities may be riding, being counted among scholars, or living in Basra. The general verb is sufficient for a formal grammatical analysis, but a particular verb may explain the sentence more exactly. This suggests that a predicative phrase can remain predicative even when the semantic governor is particular.\n\nThe glossator objects that the distinction between approaches is not merely verbal. Some expressions require a specific supplied action, such as “who will guarantee the refined text for you?” A bare “being” cannot reproduce their meaning. It is possible to reconcile the rival claims if “general existential governor” describes the most usual case rather than an exclusive rule.\n\nIn four syntactic positions—predicate, relative clause, descriptive adjective and circumstance—grammarians commonly require an understood general governor to be suppressed, since the context already indicates it and the adverbial takes its place. Ibn Jinnī reportedly permits its overt expression, but al-Raḍī rejects the alleged supporting evidence. The verse containing “settled with him” can be interpreted as resting motionless rather than simply existing. Where an expressed existential word does occur in a revered sermon or verse, it may denote a particular form of indwelling or rest rather than unrestricted being. Other poetic exceptions are treated as necessity or similarly particular meanings.\n\nOutside those four positions, a prepositional expression is ordinarily related to an explicit governor, according to al-Raḍī. If the intended activity is specific, it may be omitted when an indicator permits recovery: “I prayed in the mosque and Zayd [prayed] in the bathhouse—which of our prayers is preferable?” Finally, some scholars prefer to rename laghw “particular” and mustaqarr “general,” to avoid calling Qurʾānic or prophetic wording “idle.” Al-Sakkākī's preference for calling the terminal rhythm of Qurʾānic verses “verse-endings” rather than “rhymed prose” offers a parallel concern for decorum.",
